and a-rollin hey guys welcome to the  making of Mario Kart skate so this all  started a little over a year ago when I  actually got one of these GoPro Gamble's  and it was amazing and I rode around on  a longboard I was like this is so much  fun then I switched it up to 60 frames  per second I was like wow so smooth I  was like I gotta make a video with that  so this would be a lot easier I didn't  actually have to push myself on a  longboard and lo and behold there's a  few companies out there now that make  long boards and the best one is called  boosted they make this electric  longboard that'll go like 20 miles an  hour  super fun super fast Sani if you turn  left really hard I can fall out just  enough I'll owe you that's something you  don't want to do you keep testing it I'm  gonna do some swerves your number one  tip for driving when you're doing these  videos not hit your person trying to  shoot them not here  always assume that the rider has no idea  where the car is I do not personally  endorse what I'm doing I do not  recommend anyone else does this so the  company called Fayette tech actually  makes these gimbals and they're so great  because they're so small so powerful and  keep your footage super steady like I'd  be doing all kinds of like this and you  may notice the camera hasn't moved at  all just the position has changed and  one day I stuck this on a PVC pipe and  you like this big long movie I was like  whoa this changes everything  ideally at each location you get one  going forward one going backwards and  maybe a couple variations in between so  one of the reasons why I wanted to shoot  this at 60 frames per seconds to really  convey the sense of speed that you get  when you are moving that fast it becomes  a little less cinematic sure but it also  becomes way more like a video game  because you get more visual information  as you're going along it does at 24  frames per second one object will be  here in the next frame will be way over  here or is at 60 frames per second it's  here here here you get a lot more  temporal resolution if you will it's  higher resolution in time so why did I  choose to be Luigi over Mario well for  one I'm the only person around here who  can ride a longboard so I needed to be  in the video too I don't look anything  like Mario but I do kind of resemble  Luigi so it made sense to do Luigi and  he's you know I guess the fan favorite  and half of this we need to do some hand  signals here since we're not dealing  with radios the challenge the struggle  in the results for a visual effects oh  my god so so the post-production process  on this video was quite a lot more  extensive than a typical video for one  60fps means two and a half times the  number of frames to render to wide  field-of-view gopro images all  really really hard to track we spent  probably two weeks just tracking the  footage so for the visual effects in  this piece I actually use two different  workflows one is all like the titles in  the big house or symbol yada yada those  were all done an element 3d version 2  from Video Copilot I was able to get it  to look good enough without having to  kick it out to a 3d program to actually  get real ray traced shadows and lighting  and all that but in my opinion the real  magic of the visual effects in this  video are due to this renderer called  octane instead of traditional renderers  that use the cpu to render stuff this  uses the GPU which has thousands of  cores versus like six or eight if you  think about programs like fume effects  or real flow as being you know fluid  simulations octane is a light simulator  so an example that made octane great for  this project is the fact that I was able  to model the simple geometry of the  environment and project textures from  the video onto the walls and that made  it so that you got realistic reflections  and you'll start seeing color from the  walls and the environment bleed into the  green color of the shell if you want to  really in-depth breakdown of what octane  can do check out this video by Kyle  Natal from mega steak man you did the  Game of Thrones Zelda intro if you're  interested in actually trying out this  renderer you can go to their website  right now and try out a free demo in  your web browser play around with the  settings and if you eventually like it I  don't get it absolutely what I do so  because I'm an idiot and can't design  these scenes better I decided to put all  the cool visual effects behind me which  requires rotoscoping and rotoscoping is  the act of cutting out a figure each and  every frame to make sure that whatever  cg stuff you put in the shot is actually  behind your character so what I did for  that was I actually sent out a call to  action on Twitter asking if anyone want  to help out with this video so thank you  so much to the rotoscoping army we got  Adam Natalie Mike Colin Andrew Brendan  Blaine and Christian I really honestly  have done it without you guys so another  person who dedicated a lot of time  helping out on this project is John from  Studio booth nail on top of doing all  the work for the crazy bridge sequence  halfway through the video he also  modeled the shell the Goomba the Bullet  Bill the swamp this is John Studio who  failed he helped out with all the  effects modeled a bunch of stuff and it  was really sweet I couldn't have done it  without him thank you said hey you want  to see Mario destroy a house so John  made those models he sent them off to me  and I animated him rendered him an  octane and positive them back into the  shot mix in all the element 3d titles  I got there's so many effects in this  video I can't think of all of them and  there's so many more effects to do we're  filming this like a week before I'm even  finished with the video I'm going on  hour 20 of working straight  twenty-five hours being awake No  29 doesn't really matter at a certain  point what what does matter what matters  so thank you very much to the artist  savant for letting us use the music  Vario in this video I actually ended up  slicing up the song and put those pieces  to different parts of the video where it  made the most sense and as long as you  cut the song on the beats and have them  blend it won't sound like it's a cut up  song even though it is so a video like  this that is so music central you almost  kind of wonder is there any need for  sound design and the answer is yes but  very subtly so Charmichael layered and  different sound elements like skateboard  wheels screeching tires whooshes a  little bit of Rumble here and there and  once you get that all kinda like mixed  in as if there's no music at all then  you can dial it back to a really subtle  level it may sound like a lot of extra  work but it's almost required for a good  sound design so thank you very much for  watching through this whole making a  video and watching the main channel  video I really appreciate it I kind of  spent my entire soul on this so I'm kind  of just you know gone for the rest of  eternity so this video was actually made  possible by the people who support us on  patreon and if you're interested in  supporting us for future videos you  would surely appreciate it  so also if you want to hit me up all my  social media things are Rendell Reaper  hit me up on Twitter I'm very active  there I see all your comments thanks for  watching  this videos done it's exciting it's  finally done actually it's not done  right now I still got another week but  when you see this will be done ideally
 

